{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-267",
  "title": "African Telecom",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "reviewing",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "African Telecom"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The telecommunications landscape of West Africa represents one of the most dramatic technological transformations witnessed in the developing world over the past century. From the colonial-era telegraph lines designed primarily for administrative control to today's rapidly expanding mobile networks enabling digital financial services and internet connectivity, West Africa's telecommunications infrastructure has undergone profound changes that mirror the region's political, economic, and social evolution. This comprehensive analysis examines the historical development, current state, and ongoing challenges facing internet and telecommunications infrastructure across West Africa, revealing how technological advancement has both reflected and driven broader developmental changes in the region."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Colonial Origins and Early Infrastructure Development"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The Foundation of Control-Oriented Communications"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The development of telecommunications infrastructure in West Africa began during the colonial era, when European powers established communication systems primarily to maintain administrative control over their territories[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). The colonial telecommunications model was fundamentally extractive in nature, designed to facilitate communication between colonial capitals and their administrative centers within Africa, while deliberately precluding intra-African telecommunications that might challenge colonial economic structures[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). This approach systematically destroyed pre-existing intra-African trading systems and communication networks, replacing them with centralized systems that served colonial interests rather than local development needs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In Ghana, one of the most well-documented cases of early telecommunications development in West Africa, the colonial administration began laying the foundation for modern communications infrastructure in the late 19th century[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). The first telegraph line was established in 1882, stretching 2.5 miles between Christianborg and Accra, marking the beginning of what would become Ghana's national telecommunications network[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). Between 1887 and 1889, these telegraph lines were systematically extended to cover major colonial administrative centers including Accra, Prampram, Winneba, Saltpond, Sekondi, Ankobra, Dixcove, and Shama—all locations that served as colonial castles, fort towns, commercial ports, and fishing centers[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The expansion of telecommunications infrastructure into the interior regions of West African territories followed colonial military and administrative needs rather than economic development priorities. In Ghana, telegraph lines were extended to the middle and northern parts of the territory in 1886, reaching into Ashanti lands[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). The strategic importance of these communications lines became evident during the Yaa Asantewa war between 1900 and 1901, when the new communications technology was actively used to coordinate military operations against the Ashantis[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). This pattern of telecommunications serving primarily political and military control functions, rather than economic development or social connectivity, characterized the colonial approach throughout West Africa."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Technical Infrastructure and Local Resistance"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The physical implementation of early telecommunications infrastructure faced significant challenges from local populations who viewed the new technology with suspicion and resistance. In Ghana, telegraph lines were frequently cut down by locals who were convinced that the cables were \"magic\" lines being used by Europeans to win wars[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). This resistance forced colonial administrators to adapt their implementation strategies, leading Ghana's colonial governor to entrust the safety and security of telegraph lines to tribal chiefs in 1886, offering handsome rewards for reporting any damage to the infrastructure[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The gradual expansion of telephone services followed the establishment of telegraph networks, with the first manual telephone exchange of seventy lines installed in Accra in 1892[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). This was followed by a second manual exchange of thirteen lines in Cape Coast in 1904, demonstrating the slow but steady expansion of telecommunications capabilities[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). By the end of 1912, Ghana had constructed 1,492 miles of telegraph lines linking forty-eight telegraph offices throughout the country, creating a comprehensive communications network that served colonial administrative needs[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The period between World War I and 1920 marked a significant expansion in telephone infrastructure, with the construction of the backbone trunk telephone routes connecting Accra-Takoradi, Accra-Kumasi, Kumasi-Takoradi, and Kumasi-Tamale using unshielded copper wires[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). By 1930, the number of telephone exchange lines in Ghana had grown to 1,560, effectively linking the coastal region with the central and northern parts of the country and establishing the foundation for national telecommunications infrastructure that would persist well into the post-independence era[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Post-Independence Development and Regional Cooperation Initiatives"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "National Telecommunications Modernization"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The attainment of independence across West Africa in the late 1950s and early 1960s brought new dynamism to telecommunications development, as newly independent nations sought to modernize their inherited colonial infrastructure and develop communications systems that served national rather than colonial interests[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). In Ghana, independence in 1957 triggered the launch of a seven-year development plan that accelerated telecommunications expansion, leading to the completion of a second automatic telephone exchange in Accra and the rapid growth of subscriber numbers to more than 16,000 by 1963, with 32,000 rotary-type telephones in use[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The rapid growth in commercial activities across West Africa during the post-independence period, including mining, timber, cocoa, and other primary commodity exports, created new demands for telecommunications infrastructure in outlying regions[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). This economic expansion drove the installation of new manual exchanges at various locations including Cantoments, Accra, Swedru, Koforidua, Ho, Tamale, Sunyani, and Kumasi, using Strowger (step-by-step) and Philip UR 49 type switches[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf). The management structure of telecommunications also evolved during this period, with Ghana transferring telecommunications administration from the Public Works Department to the post office following the enactment of the Post Office Ordinance in 1886, and later to the government's Post and Telecommunications Department until the early 1970s[2](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Ghana.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The Pan-African Telecommunications Vision"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The most ambitious attempt to transform West Africa's telecommunications landscape during the post-independence era was the Pan-African Telecommunications Network (Panaftel), conceived as a continental network that would revolutionize intra-African communications[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). The genesis of Panaftel can be traced to the 1962 meeting of the Regional Plan Committee for Africa in Dakar, Senegal, where 115 delegates sought to fundamentally redefine the communications structure established during the colonial period[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). The delegates recognized that the colonial system had directed no attention or resources toward intra-African communications capabilities, instead focusing exclusively on connections between African territories and their respective colonial metropoles."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the time of Panaftel's conception in 1962, intra-African telecommunications infrastructure was severely underdeveloped, with only fifteen HF radio systems operating between African countries, one UHF radio system operating in East Africa (Kenya, Tanzania, and Uganda), and thirteen land cable systems carrying approximately 100 circuits between African countries[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). The Committee delegates envisioned a continental network that would eliminate the need to transmit intra-African communications through non-African transit centers and would facilitate the achievement of African political, economic, and social unity through improved communications infrastructure[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The development of Panaftel proceeded through several phases, with the second meeting of the African Plan Committee occurring in 1967 in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, where the network's structure was further elaborated and concrete implementation steps were established[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). In 1968, the International Telecommunications Union (ITU) requested funding from the United Nations Development Program (UNDP) for Panaftel's preinvestment surveys, leading to preliminary studies of thirty-eight African countries completed in 1969[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). The UNDP subsequently granted $2 million to the ITU for detailed technical, economic, and financial studies, which concluded that a network combining coaxial cable and microwave radio-relay links would be optimal for Panaftel[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The original Panaftel specifications were ambitious, envisioning a network with 20,000 kilometers of transmission arteries and eighteen international switching centers, with routes selected to serve as national backbones connecting major population centers within countries while providing international links between neighboring countries[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf). However, the estimated cost of $115 million proved challenging for many African nations to support, and the project's implementation faced significant obstacles related to political instability, lack of indigenous technical expertise, and insufficient financial resources[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The Mobile Revolution and GSM Implementation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Nigeria's Telecommunications Transformation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The most significant transformation in West African telecommunications infrastructure occurred with the introduction of the Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) in the early 2000s, with Nigeria serving as a pioneering example of rapid mobile adoption and network expansion[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). Prior to GSM implementation, Nigeria's telecommunications landscape was characterized by extremely limited access, with only a few thousand landline connections managed by the state-owned Nigerian Telecommunications Limited (NITEL)[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). This situation changed dramatically in 2001 when the Nigerian Communications Commission (NCC) awarded GSM licenses to three key operators through a transparent bidding process: MTN Nigeria, Econet Wireless (now Airtel Nigeria), and NITEL through its subsidiary M-Tel[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The licensing process demonstrated the anticipated potential of the Nigerian telecom market, with MTN and Econet Wireless each paying $285 million for their licenses[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). MTN Nigeria became the first operator to launch services in August 2001, followed by Econet Wireless and later M-Tel[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). Initially, GSM services were positioned as luxury products, with SIM cards costing up to ₦20,000, but the immense demand prompted rapid network expansion and service upgrades[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). This high initial demand also attracted additional investment and established the foundation for sustained competition and innovation in the Nigerian telecommunications market."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The competitive landscape intensified significantly with the arrival of additional operators, particularly Globacom (Glo) in 2003, which introduced revolutionary pricing strategies including free SIM cards, a first in Nigeria[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). Glo's market entry forced existing operators to lower prices, making GSM services more affordable and accessible to broader segments of the population[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). In 2008, Etisalat (now 9mobile) joined the market with a focus on data services and targeting Nigeria's younger, tech-savvy demographic[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). The addition of these new players intensified competition, drove down prices, and expanded GSM coverage to rural and underserved areas, fundamentally transforming telecommunications access across Nigeria."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Economic Impact and Infrastructure Development"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The introduction of GSM technology in Nigeria and across West Africa has had transformative economic impacts that extend far beyond the telecommunications sector itself[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). The rapid expansion of mobile networks created entirely new economic opportunities, including mobile banking services, e-commerce platforms, and digital payment systems that have revolutionized financial inclusion across the region. The competitive market structure established through multiple operators has driven continuous innovation in service delivery, pricing strategies, and network coverage expansion, particularly in rural and previously underserved areas."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The infrastructure development associated with GSM implementation required massive capital investments in tower infrastructure, switching centers, and transmission networks across West Africa. The involvement of international equipment vendors and tower infrastructure companies created technology transfer opportunities and local capacity building initiatives that have strengthened the region's telecommunications capabilities. The Nigerian Communications Commission's regulatory oversight has played a crucial role in maintaining competitive markets, ensuring service quality standards, and promoting continued investment in network expansion and modernization."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Current Infrastructure Challenges and Digital Divide"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Rural Connectivity and Access Barriers"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Despite the remarkable progress achieved through mobile network expansion, significant challenges remain in achieving universal telecommunications and internet access across West Africa. Rural connectivity continues to present major obstacles, with network coverage often concentrated in urban and economically viable areas while leaving remote and sparsely populated regions underserved. The economic challenges of extending infrastructure to these areas, combined with lower population densities and reduced purchasing power, create sustainable business model challenges for telecommunications operators seeking to achieve universal coverage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The digital divide between urban and rural areas is further exacerbated by differences in digital literacy, device affordability, and the availability of relevant local content and services. While mobile phone adoption has reached impressive levels across West Africa, internet usage and data services adoption remain more limited, particularly in rural areas where infrastructure quality may be insufficient to support reliable high-speed data services. The transition from basic voice and SMS services to more advanced internet-based applications requires not only improved infrastructure but also enhanced digital skills and more affordable devices capable of accessing advanced services."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Regulatory and Policy Framework Development"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The evolution of telecommunications infrastructure in West Africa has required the development of sophisticated regulatory frameworks capable of managing competitive markets, ensuring service quality, promoting investment, and addressing emerging challenges such as cybersecurity and data protection. The Nigerian Communications Commission's experience in managing the transition from state monopoly to competitive markets provides valuable insights for other West African countries seeking to modernize their telecommunications sectors[4](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/evolution-gsm-nigeria-comprehensive-overview-christian-omeni-bnpcf). Effective regulation must balance the need to promote competition and innovation with requirements for universal service obligations and infrastructure sharing arrangements that can improve coverage in economically challenging areas."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Regional cooperation initiatives continue to play important roles in addressing cross-border telecommunications challenges, harmonizing regulatory approaches, and promoting the development of integrated regional networks that can support intra-African communications and internet connectivity. The lessons learned from earlier initiatives like Panaftel provide valuable guidance for contemporary efforts to develop regional telecommunications infrastructure that can support economic integration and social development across West Africa[1](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Conclusion"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The evolution of internet and telecommunications infrastructure in West Africa represents a remarkable transformation from colonial-era systems designed for administrative control to modern mobile networks enabling economic empowerment and social connectivity. The region's experience demonstrates both the potential for rapid technological adoption and the persistent challenges of achieving universal access and bridging digital divides. The success of GSM implementation, particularly in countries like Nigeria, illustrates how competitive market structures and appropriate regulatory frameworks can drive rapid infrastructure development and service innovation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Looking forward, the continued development of telecommunications infrastructure in West Africa will require sustained investment in both physical networks and human capacity development, along with innovative approaches to extending connectivity to underserved areas. The region's experience with mobile-first development strategies and leapfrogging traditional telecommunications technologies provides valuable lessons for other developing regions, while ongoing challenges related to rural connectivity, digital literacy, and infrastructure sustainability require continued attention from policymakers, industry stakeholders, and development partners. The transformation of West Africa's telecommunications landscape from colonial control mechanisms to engines of economic development and social connectivity demonstrates the profound potential for technology to drive positive change when supported by appropriate policies, competitive markets, and sustained investment in infrastructure development."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. [https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf](https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/imce-uploads/CITI/Articles/Models%20for%20Development.pdf)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
